module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_drops_rederivation_instrument_sits_in_the_population_it_measures

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread, BlockerClassUnstated }

data a_drops_rederivation_instrument_sits_in_the_population_it_measures: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_drops_rederivation_instrument_sits_in_the_population_it_measures" as NonEmptyStr,
  rung: RungUnread { cause: BlockerClassUnstated },

  receipts: [
    "**a declared drop is bounded by a census, and the census is itself a module of the population the drop covers, so the population can break the instrument that would count it and nothing goes red.** INVALID STATE: gunbc.rung_drop v1_required_lanes_withdrawn names its population as what gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:required-lane-resolution-census enumerates, and that instrument's entry (gunbc.required_lane_resolution_census_live) imports modules that are outside the two emitted closures, so a refusal in any of them stops the instrument at resolve before it prints a single module. The drop's re-derivation then answers nothing, while the drop still reads as bounded.",

    "HARM. The drop becomes unbounded with nothing red. The required job v2-bootstrap judges only the two emitted closures, so it stays green when the census's own closure stops resolving; the census is not a required context, so its failure is a dispatch-time exit and nobody is looking. A reader who cites the drop as a bounded population is citing a number no instrument can currently produce.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS, both measured by executed runs of the dispatched instrument (instrument-dispatch, label //gunbc/instruments:required-lane-resolution-census, exit 1 both times, no module list printed). At main 0ac3102d1b07 (run 39387690147): resolve failed for dag/gunbc/required_lane_resolution_census_live.dag at dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_authority.dag:2989 with call shape mismatch calling updated, argument on and argument note each supplied more than once. At main 585d918af6c3 (run 39389715573), after that line was repaired: dag/extdeps/github/expressions.dag:702:9 non-exhaustive match, missing variant LogicalNot, and dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_event_carrier.dag:444:117 type mismatch, expected Int, got RoadmapEventId. Over the same interval the required job v2-bootstrap succeeded at 585d918af6c3 (run 39385184965): it records each emitted closure only as a closure_identity hash and an emitted file count, not as a module list, so the closures the drop is defined against exist only inside the job. This differs from a_census_is_cited_wider_than_the_closure_its_instrument_compiles, whose reading is present and correct but cited too wide, and from an_instrument_resolves_wider_than_the_subject_it_measures, whose instrument dies on cost; here the instrument is refused on correctness, by modules it was supposed to be counting.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACT, closure record: the required job records each emitted closure only as a closure_identity hash plus an emitted file count (v2-bootstrap run 39385184965: self-host 4e519e8f, 249 files; v2-native-cli 6478154a, 240 files; no artifact), never the module list. The drop's coverage therefore cannot be checked from the job's own record: whether the census closure lies inside either emitted closure is not decidable without re-deriving the closures by a separate run.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable. The break is found only when someone dispatches the census by hand. CEILING: MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE, and this row said structurally guaranteed until the parent rejected that wording at head 20a75dc4cdc. Mechanical prevention here is two obligations, (1) the join must be judged by the binary the required job emits, so host Rust cannot wire it: it needs the `v2.std.decl_index` `DeclFact` initializer projection to carry bound names, so that the join can be expressed in .dag and emitted, and a REQUIRED context must then run that join (today the host join is `v1_compiler.cli_run.rostered_row_join` `run_rostered_row_join`) and FAIL on an Err or on a non-empty JoinReport.findings -- NOT on a non-empty JoinReport, which a healthy run has: JoinReport carries counts and, for fixture-homed enrolled types, control_findings that are EXPECTED to fire (`JoinReport.control_findings`; `JoinFindingKind::ControlDidNotFire` exists to refuse a control that stayed silent). findings are refusals from production rosters and from the join's own integrity; control findings and counts keep their own roles, so a wiring that tested the whole report would refuse every healthy run; (2) the census this drop is bounded by must be judged on that required path, so a refusal anywhere in its closure fails a required context instead of surfacing as a dispatch-time exit nobody reads. Structural impossibility really is out of reach while the drop's population is deliberately larger than what the required job compiles, because that gap IS the drop; that is not a licence to claim structural now, and (1) is parked behind the named capability, not behind a caller alone.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMED AS A CAPABILITY: the `DeclFact` initializer projection must carry bound names so the join can be expressed in .dag and emitted. The host join has no caller at all, and a required-context caller of run_rostered_row_join is not the repair, because the join must be judged by the emitted binary. Measured at this head: grep -rn 'run_rostered_row_join(' --include=*.rs over the checkout returns EXACTLY ONE line, `run_rostered_row_join` in `v1_compiler.cli_run.rostered_row_join`, and that line is the DEFINITION (pub fn run_rostered_row_join(index: &DeclarationIndex) -> Result<JoinReport, String>). A call cannot be spelled without the open paren, so that single hit IS the absence of a caller: the function is never called, in production or in its own test module. The only other mentions anywhere in the checkout are prose. No workflow dispatches it, no instrument_targets entry reaches it, no shipped claim imports it, and the module's own test module exercises the inner file-to-roster arms rather than this entry point. So the sentence that `gunbc.derived_row_roster_seed_growth` `reason` carried until this head, that the file-versus-roster join arms 'execute inside run_rostered_row_join on the required parse path (witnesses lane)', stated a consumer that is absent from the tree, and it was the sentence that let the hole read as covered; this head corrects that source to say no required context calls the join. THE TRIGGER IS THEREFORE THE CAPABILITY: the `DeclFact` initializer projection carrying bound names, so the join is expressed in .dag, emitted, and judged by the emitted binary; the wiring is parked behind it. Once it lands, a DeclaredNotRostered, RowFileNotRostered or RosterMemberNotARowFile finding (real JoinFindingKind variants in `v1_compiler.cli_run.rostered_row_join`) fails the required context. The mutation nimble-badger-800 reproduced in PR comment 6289242275 -- renaming only the identity: field of THIS row, its own words COMPILE[renamed] rc=0 outputs=23, '0 blocking error(s)', nothing shipped going red -- evidences ONLY the interface it exercised, nimble-badger-800's private verifier claim -- a claim that is not in the repo, so nothing here can re-execute it or state what it reads. It does NOT discriminate the Rust join, which never reads that field: the DeclaredNotRostered arm keys on the declaration identity (row.module_path, row.decl_name) and the file arms key on file membership, so a renamed identity: passes the join untouched. A RED that would discriminate the join plants what the join keys on: a row .dag file the roster does not name (RowFileNotRostered), or a roster member with no sibling .dag file (RosterMemberNotARowFile), or a declared row absent from the roster (DeclaredNotRostered). That is the RED the wiring owes. Charter 6 (the native compiler judging the withdrawn population) remains the trigger for the SECOND obligation, the census being judged natively on the required path; it is explicitly NOT a prerequisite for the first. NOT satisfied by an author re-running the census, or the join, by hand after each break: that is the mode that let two SHAs pass unobserved.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
